<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Roland Brownlee - Full Pack Part 1</title></head><body><h1>Roland Brownlee - Full Pack Part 1</h1><p>Roland Brownlee — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 1)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>03-04 Process Optimisation</p><p>Plaud file id: 8c5d5f11a9f9a7ecb3a739a83e0dab06</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance Review and Process Optimisation for Roland Brownlee</p><p>This transcript captures a performance review meeting between Bob Duncan (manager) and Roland Brownlee (advisor), with Megan Gordon (admin) contributing. The discussion covers Roland&#x27;s lead performance from the previous month, an analysis of his current business pipeline, and a deep dive into data integrity and workflow process adherence. Key outcomes include identifying and correcting data entry errors, setting a goal to improve insurance conversion rates, and tasking Roland with analysing non-converting leads to improve overall efficiency.</p><p>## Review of Previous Month&#x27;s Lead Performance</p><p>Bob Duncan initiated the review by analysing Roland&#x27;s lead generation statistics for the preceding month. The data revealed a total of 21 leads allocated to Roland. From this pool, conversion metrics indicated that four opportunities successfully transitioned into business, with one currently at the sign-up stage and three at the &#x27;offer received&#x27; stage. Bob noted that while these figures represent secured business, they do not capture the entirety of Roland&#x27;s activity.</p><p>The review also highlighted the status of the remaining leads. A few are still marked as &#x27;appointment booked&#x27;, while the &#x27;Not Proceeding With&#x27; (NPW) category remains relatively low, containing only one lead alongside one client who was not interested. Additionally, four leads have moved into &#x27;stale lead&#x27; status. Bob emphasised that while the current conversion numbers are positive, there is a significant portion of leads that are not progressing, setting the stage for a later discussion on lead quality and conversion strategies.</p><p>## Data Integrity and Pipeline Management</p><p>A significant portion of the meeting addressed operational hygiene, specifically regarding data entry and pipeline accuracy. An immediate issue was identified with a self-generated lead for a client named Lindsay Boyle, which Roland had submitted that morning. Megan Gordon flagged that the lead was added incorrectly, missing the specific staff name required for proper reporting and commission tracking. To rectify this, Roland was instructed to &#x27;Not Take Up&#x27; (NTU) the current application and re-submit it with the correct &#x27;Introducers&#x27; lead source and staff code to ensure he gets paid.</p><p>The discussion then broadened to Roland&#x27;s overall pipeline, which currently holds 29 applications in progress, equating to £20,500 in mortgage business and £7,000 in fees. However, gaps in data were noted, specifically missing &#x27;on-risk&#x27; dates for clients Claire Bet and Gary Linton. Bob stressed the importance of these dates for accurate income projection, instructing Roland to input estimated dates—even if they are placeholders for three months down the line—so that the system can forecast income correctly. Additionally, a &#x27;blue case&#x27; involving a buy-to-let for a brother was identified as not proceeding due to credit score failure and client disengagement; Roland agreed to NTU this case immediately.</p><p>## Analysis of Overall Performance and Future Goal Setting</p><p>Bob evaluated Roland&#x27;s performance against broader business targets, noting that while he was slightly disappointed that colleague Paul had secured more written business recently, Roland remains on a strong trajectory. With £14,700 achieved in February and a pipeline suggesting he is on track for his £45,000 quarterly target, confidence in his performance remains high. Roland&#x27;s insurance attachment rate is currently sitting at 33% for the quarter, but his year-to-date figure is a healthy 45.5%.</p><p>A key highlight was Roland&#x27;s &quot;cracking&quot; average premium, which Bob noted is well above the required level and could realistically support a £300,000 revenue year if maintained. To push performance further, Bob proposed a specific goal for Roland to achieve a 50% insurance conversion rate for March and April, which Roland accepted. Financially, Roland&#x27;s banking income has risen in line with his business levels, with just shy of £1,700 banked in the previous week, positioning him well to enter bonus territory for the quarter.</p><p>## Workflow Adherence and Lead Conversion Strategy</p><p>The meeting shifted focus to process efficiency, particularly the use of the &#x27;Platform&#x27; system. Bob pointed out that while Roland had only a few overdue tasks—one sign-up from eight days ago and some upcoming appointments—proper workflow adherence is critical. Unlike the previous &#x27;Midas&#x27; system, &#x27;Platform&#x27; relies on the advisor completing specific tasks to trigger subsequent administrative actions for Megan, such as chasing documents. Bob urged Roland to adopt this habit to offload administrative burdens and focus on advising.</p><p>A strategic analysis of lead conversion followed. Bob highlighted that out of 196 total leads received since Roland started, 108 have moved into NPW statuses, meaning roughly 55% of leads are not converting. While Roland converts approximately one in two clients he actually speaks to (49%), the high volume of non-contactable or stale leads is a concern. To address this, Bob tasked Roland with analysing these 108 non-converting leads to identify common trends—such as the source or booking method—to determine if lead quality or booking processes need adjustment. The objective is to reduce the &#x27;wastage&#x27; number rather than simply increasing lead volume, thereby improving overall efficiency.</p><p>## System Issues and Inter-Team Collaboration</p><p>Technical frustrations were raised regarding the &#x27;Platform&#x27; and &#x27;Teams&#x27; software. Roland reported a specific issue where the &#x27;Platform&#x27; system incorrectly displays his availability for face-to-face appointments in &#x27;Glasgow South Side and West End&#x27; instead of his actual branch, &#x27;Newton Mearns&#x27;. Bob acknowledged this error, noting he couldn&#x27;t amend it himself, and agreed to raise a support ticket to rectify the branch listing.</p><p>Despite these technical hitches, the working relationship between Roland and Megan was reported as positive. Megan confirmed that they are establishing a good routine, with Roland passing cases over efficiently and Megan picking them up largely within the same or next day. The meeting concluded on a high note, with Bob acknowledging that the trajectory is positive and encouraging Roland to aim for the top spot in performance for the current month.</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Roland**</p><p>- [ ] NTU (Not Take Up) the Lindsay Boyle application and re-submit with the correct lead source and staff name. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Add a note to the re-submitted application stating: &quot;I&#x27;ve had to NTU it to ensure lead source was correct&quot;. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] NTU the &#x27;blue case&#x27; (brother&#x27;s buy-to-let) that is not proceeding. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Update the status of the protection case that has new terms and a delayed entry date. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Add an estimated &#x27;on-risk&#x27; date for Claire Bet (currently missing). - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Add an estimated &#x27;on-risk&#x27; date for Gary Linton, projected for three months down the line. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure every active case has an estimated &#x27;on-risk&#x27; date to support income projection. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Review the 108 NPW (Not Proceeding With) leads to identify common trends or reasons for non-conversion. - [Next Week]</p><p>- [ ] Update all overdue workflow tasks in the system to ensure admin triggers are fired. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Keep Bob informed regarding the status of the ongoing Teams voicemail issue. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Aim for a 50% insurance conversion rate for March and April. - [April]</p><p>**@Bob**</p><p>- [ ] Raise a support ticket to correct Roland&#x27;s branch location on &#x27;Platform&#x27; from &quot;West End&quot; to &quot;Newton Mearns&quot;. - [TBD]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Internal Performance Review — Roland Brownlee (Creation date: 2026-03-04)</p><p>### 1. Follow-up From Previous Meeting (only where referenced)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **On-risk / projected dates still missing on some cases**, despite being raised in the previous review (“you mentioned that last week as well”).</p><p>- Missing dates are impacting **projected income reporting** accuracy.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week</p><p>- **Stagnant**: The same reporting gap (missing dates) remains.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Megan is proactively updating case records when flagged (e.g., **John Betts updated during the call**).</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Repeated behaviour**: Estimated/projection dates are not being added when exact dates aren’t known.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Revenue forecasting risk**: Cases without on-risk/projected dates won’t fall into the correct month in reporting, reducing visibility and management support.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Add **estimated on-risk/projected dates** where unknown (use a **3-month estimate** unless a confirmed date exists), then pull forward once confirmed.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Roland)** for setting estimated dates</p><p>- **Admin (Megan)** for updating when dates are confirmed</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — same reporting discipline issue recurring week-to-week.</p><p>---</p><p>### 2. Leads Performance Review (with CRM Discipline) — Last Month</p><p>#### Current Position (Last Month)</p><p>- **21 leads total**</p><p>- Outcomes from those 21:</p><p>  - **4 converted to business**</p><p>    - **1 at “Sign Up”**</p><p>    - **3 at “Offer Received”**</p><p>  - **1 NPW** (not proceeding/worked)</p><p>  - **1 “Client not interested”**</p><p>  - **4 moved to “Stale lead”**</p><p>  - Several appointments still booked (Roland confirmed some are due **2026-03-04** and **2026-03-06**)</p><p>#### Vs Last Month</p><p>- Not available (no earlier month figures stated).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- **Low NPW volume** (only 1 noted from last month’s 21).</p><p>- Appointment pipeline looks active with near-term bookings.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Lead source CRM accuracy risk** identified:</p><p>  - **Lindsay Boyle** added with incorrect/missing **staff name** and incorrect lead source configuration.</p><p>  - If not corrected, the case can be **excluded from reporting and payments**.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Payment/revenue risk** if lead source fields aren’t entered correctly (case may not bank correctly for remuneration).</p><p>- **Reporting risk**: mis-coded lead source/branch prevents accurate tracking and could understate performance.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- **Lindsay Boyle**: **NTU and re-add** case with correct Lead Source setup:</p><p>  - Lead source type: **Introducers**</p><p>  - Lead source: **“Ready and Go” (correct code)**</p><p>  - Lead source branch: correct introducer branch</p><p>  - Staff name: **Roland Brownlee** (if self-gen) / correct rep code (if referral)</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving** on lead outcomes (low NPW, multiple offers), but **CRM discipline is a recurring weak point** with direct pay/reporting impact.</p><p>---</p><p>### 3. Lead-to-Business Conversion (Behaviour + Quality Signal)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Total leads since start: **196**</p><p>- **108** moved into NPW statuses (≈ **55%** of total)</p><p>- Business pipeline outcomes:</p><p>  - **36 applications**</p><p>  - **12 completed**</p><p>  - **18%** of all leads convert to business (12 / 196)</p><p>- If NPWs excluded: conversion is **~49% (almost 1 in 2)** once cases are “in front of you”.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- **Strong conversion when engaged**: ~1 in 2 once prospects are actually progressing.</p><p>- Indicates advising/closing capability is **not the problem** when contact is established.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **High NPW volume** suggests:</p><p>  - too many low-intent appointments being booked, or</p><p>  - insufficient pre-qualification / confirmation, or</p><p>  - inconsistent follow-up discipline before/after booking.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If lead flow increases without improving NPW rate, workload increases but **conversion volume may not rise proportionally**.</p><p>- Time loss from no-shows / weak-intent bookings reduces capacity for protection and progressing cases.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Identify common traits of NPW cases (e.g., source type, booking method, confirmation behaviour) and agree a simple fix:</p><p>  - confirmation call/text approach,</p><p>  - tighter pre-qualification standards,</p><p>  - clearer “mutually agreed time” rule before diary booking.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Roland)** with input from **Other (Lead gen team/Weems Place if applicable)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — conversion is strong *when contacted*, but the **top-of-funnel leakage (NPW)** remains the limiting factor.</p><p>---</p><p>### 4. Applications in Progress</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **29 applications**</p><p>- **£20.5k** mortgage business</p><p>- **£7k** in fees</p><p>- Majority are **at/near “Offered”** stages.</p><p>- **1 “blue” case** flagged as unlikely to proceed:</p><p>  - Scenario: purchase of brother’s buy-to-let; failed credit score; client did not provide Experian report; client appears to have disengaged.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week</p><p>- Not available (no prior case count stated).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- High proportion of cases progressing to **offer**.</p><p>- Admin support working well: Megan is picking up cases same/next day.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Ownership closure**: the non-proceeding case needs to be cleaned up (NTU/close) to prevent pipeline distortion.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Pipeline accuracy risk if dead cases remain open.</p><p>- Potential delay risks where documents/medical underwriting could stall completion (see protection section).</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- **Non-proceeding “blue” case**: update/close as NTU/not proceeding with clear reason logged.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Admin (Megan)** updated during call (already actioned in-session), but ensure final status is correct and notes are complete.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving** — pipeline is largely “offered”, suggesting effective case progression.</p><p>---</p><p>### 5. Protection in Progress</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **£19k** shown in protection policies (period not specified).</p><p>- Medical underwriting delays noted:</p><p>  - **Pete (linked to Claire Betts case)**: moved in; GP follow-up required due to screening flag.</p><p>  - **Gary Linton**: GP appointment scheduled **2026-03-06**; potential high blood pressure issue pending.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week</p><p>- Not available, but Bob referenced this as “the same one from last time we spoke” being resolved.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Protection activity volume appears strong overall.</p><p>- Adviser intent stated: product transfers without fee are still expected to be supported by **insurance**.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Missing on-risk dates** for:</p><p>  - **Claire Betts**</p><p>  - **Gary Linton**</p><p>- These omissions reduce visibility of when protection income is expected to land.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Underwriting/medical delays could cause **slippage** and unpredictability in income recognition.</p><p>- Reporting gaps (missing dates) prevent accurate forecasting.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Add **projected on-risk date** (use **3 months from 2026-03-04 = 2026-06-04** as an estimate) for:</p><p>  - **Claire Betts**</p><p>  - **Gary Linton**</p><p>- Bring forward once confirmed by solicitor/admin update.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Roland)** to add the estimate</p><p>- **Admin (Megan)** to refine when confirmed</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — protection volume looks healthy, but **date discipline continues to undermine forecasting**.</p><p>---</p><p>### 6. Written Business — This Month / Quarter-to-Date (as discussed)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- February written business: **£14.7k**</p><p>- Required to stay on track for **£45k quarter**: **~£15.3k** additional (as stated in review discussion).</p><p>- Roland confidence: **uncertain** (“don’t know is the answer”), but expects pipeline to convert via AIPs and follow-ups.</p><p>#### Vs Last Month</p><p>- Not enough detail beyond February figure.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Strong belief in pipeline emerging from AIPs and follow-up discipline.</p><p>- Workflow usage improving, suggesting better repeatable output.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Adviser confidence is mixed; relies on pipeline “coming out the blue”.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Over-reliance on late-stage surprises vs. controlled pipeline progression could create end-of-month volatility.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Maintain tighter workflow progression so admin tasks trigger automatically and reduce adviser admin burden.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving** — written performance and pipeline control are trending upward, but consistency depends on workflow discipline.</p><p>---</p><p>### 7. Banked Business — This Month (as discussed)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Banked amount referenced for last week: **~£1,700** (Megan: “just shy of 1700”).</p><p>- General trend: banking income “going up in the right direction”.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Banking is aligning with increasing business volumes and supports bonus potential.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- None explicitly stated (minor uncertainty from Bob on exact weekly total, but Megan confirmed figure).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- None stated beyond general slippage risks tied to missing dates and underwriting.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- None specifically agreed in this section.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- N/A</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving** — banked income increasing with pipeline maturation.</p><p>---</p><p>### 8. Pipeline Health — Remortgage Opportunities</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Remortgage pipeline (via Stephen):</p><p>  - **6 cases for March**</p><p>  - **7 cases for April**</p><p>- Adviser intention: contact now to build rapport; expects many to convert ~6 months ahead.</p><p>- Longer-term pipeline: **80 applications** noted for next year.</p><p>- Forecast view referenced: **£45k + £13k** “good chunk of cash” sitting there (as discussed).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Proactive rapport-building approach should improve retention and conversion.</p><p>- Early engagement aligns with longer sales cycles.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- None stated; operationally depends on admin booking and adviser follow-up cadence.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If booking/calling isn’t executed consistently, early rapport strategy becomes “ongoing” work without conversion.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Ensure clients are called and diarised appropriately (bookings driven by **Anna / Megan**, per discussion).</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Other (Anna)** for calling/booking</p><p>- **Admin (Megan)** support as applicable</p><p>- **Adviser (Roland)** to maintain contact strategy</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving** — pipeline visibility is strong; success depends on consistent contact and diary management.</p><p>---</p><p>### 9. CRM Discipline &amp; Workflow Execution (Operational Performance)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Overdue workflow/task noted:</p><p>  - **One task overdue by 8 days** (overdue since **2026-02-24**), explained as not completing the task after first appointment and rescheduling.</p><p>- Workflow stage progression is not consistently updated, preventing admin automation triggers.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week</p><p>- Not quantified, but clearly ongoing given repeated reminders and process coaching.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Roland is rebooking and progressing appointments in reality.</p><p>- Megan confirms collaboration is improving and cases are being passed over effectively.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **CRM hygiene gap**: tasks not completed/moved forward even when the client meeting has happened.</p><p>- Prevents automated delegation (e.g., document chasing) from triggering to admin.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Ownership gaps**: if stages aren’t moved forward, Megan won’t receive system prompts (documents/next-step tasks), causing delays.</p><p>- **SLA risk**: slower document collection and case movement due to missing triggers.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Same-day CRM updates after appointments:</p><p>  - complete the task,</p><p>  - move workflow stage forward,</p><p>  - log next steps so admin triggers fire.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving slowly** — routine is better, but CRM/task completion remains inconsistent and creates operational drag.</p><p>---</p><p>## Master Action List</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| NTU and re-add with correct lead source fields (Introducers + Ready and Go code + correct branch + staff name) | **Lindsay Boyle** (product transfer) | Adviser (Roland) | 2026-03-04 | Excluded from reporting/banking; **risk of not being paid** |</p><p>| Add estimated **on-risk/projected dates** (use **2026-06-04** as estimate) and refine later | **Claire Betts**, **Gary Linton** | Adviser (Roland) | 2026-03-04 | Forecasting/reporting blind spot; revenue timing unclear |</p><p>| Update workflow/task completion and move stages forward after appointments (avoid overdue tasks) | Workflow discipline | Adviser (Roland) | 2026-03-07 | Admin triggers won’t fire; document chasing/case progress delays |</p><p>| Analyse NPW drivers and propose changes to reduce **~55% NPW rate** | Lead conversion process | Adviser (Roland) + Other (Lead gen team) | 2026-03-11 | Lead wastage continues; capacity lost; growth constrained |</p><p>| Raise ticket to fix face-to-face location showing wrong area (should show Newton Mearns, not Glasgow West End/South Side) | Platform settings | Other (Bob) | 2026-03-05 | Client confusion; mis-booked appointments; reputational friction |</p><p>| Keep Bob updated on Teams issue (voicemail request causing disruption) | Teams telephony | Adviser (Roland) | 2026-03-07 | Missed calls/slow response times; lead/contact risk |</p><p>---</p><p>## Email Recap (Auto-generated from Review)</p><p>**Subject:** Weekly Performance Review and Agreed Actions</p><p>Hi Roland,</p><p>Below is a summary of this week’s review and agreed actions.</p><p>## Follow-Up From Last Week</p><p>**Outstanding**</p><p>- On-risk/projected dates: still missing on some cases despite being raised previously (impacting projected income reporting).</p><p>## Performance Snapshot</p><p>- **Leads (last month):** 21 total; 4 converted to business (1 sign up, 3 offer received); 1 NPW; 4 stale leads; appointments due **2026-03-04** and **2026-03-06**.</p><p>- **Applications in progress:** 29 apps; **£20.5k** mortgage business; **£7k** fees; majority offered.</p><p>- **Protection:** £19k shown; underwriting delays noted on **Pete/Claire Betts** and **Gary Linton**.</p><p>- **Banked:** ~**£1,700** referenced for last week.</p><p>- **Conversion insight:** 196 total leads; 108 NPW (≈55%); 18% total lead-to-business; ~49% conversion once NPW excluded.</p><p>## Identified Risks</p><p>- CRM lead source coding errors can exclude cases from reporting/banking (and remuneration).</p><p>- Missing on-risk dates reduces forecast visibility and income timing accuracy.</p><p>- Workflow stages not moved forward prevents admin triggers (document chasing, next steps).</p><p>## Cases Requiring Attention</p><p>- **Lindsay Boyle** — Lead source/staff name incorrectly entered; requires NTU and re-add (Owner: Roland).</p><p>- **Claire Betts** — On-risk date missing (Owner: Roland to set estimate; Megan to refine).</p><p>- **Gary Linton** — GP appointment on **2026-03-06**; on-risk date missing (Owner: Roland to set estimate; Megan to refine).</p><p>## Focus for Next Week</p><p>- Improve workflow discipline: complete tasks and move stages forward same day after appointments.</p><p>- Eliminate reporting gaps: ensure on-risk/projected dates exist (even if estimated).</p><p>- Reduce NPW volume by identifying the common drivers and tightening booking/confirmation behaviour.</p><p>## Agreed Actions</p><p>| Action | Owner | Deadline |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| NTU and re-add **Lindsay Boyle** with correct lead source fields | Roland | 2026-03-04 |</p><p>| Add estimated on-risk/projected dates for **Claire Betts** and **Gary Linton** (then refine when confirmed) | Roland (estimate) / Megan (refine) | 2026-03-04 |</p><p>| Complete overdue tasks and move workflow stages forward after appointments | Roland | 2026-03-07 |</p><p>| Review NPW drivers and propose a change to reduce leakage | Roland (+ lead gen team input) | 2026-03-11 |</p><p>| Raise ticket to correct face-to-face location settings | Bob | 2026-03-05 |</p><p>| Keep Bob updated on Teams issue resolution | Roland | 2026-03-07 |</p><p>| If circumstances change during the week, CRM must be updated immediately and blockers flagged early. |  |  |</p><p>| Bob |  |  |</p><p>### Performance Review &amp; Goals</p><p>Subject: Detailed Catch-Up Recap &amp; Clear Goals for the Month Ahead!</p><p>Hi Roland,</p><p>It was great to connect today and review your progress. Honestly, it&#x27;s one of my easier calls when everything&#x27;s moving in the right direction, and you&#x27;re certainly making that happen!</p><p>Let&#x27;s break down the key areas we covered, celebrating your wins and setting clear targets for continued success.</p><p>### Your Performance Highlights:</p><p>- **Exceptional Conversion:** Your ability to convert leads once you&#x27;re engaged with them is outstanding. We noted that if we exclude the &#x27;Not Proceeding With&#x27; (NPW) cases, you&#x27;re converting almost 1 in 2 opportunities, sitting at a strong 49% . This clearly shows your advising and closing capabilities are top-notch when you get that direct interaction.</p><p>- **Rising Banking Income:** Your banking income is definitely on an upward trajectory, which is fantastic. Megan confirmed you banked just shy of £1,700 last week , and this growth is putting you in a great position for bonus territory this quarter .</p><p>- **Strong Average Premium:** Your average premium is &quot;cracking&quot; and well above our target . Long may that continue, as it significantly contributes to your overall figures.</p><p>- **Positive Admin Collaboration:** Megan gave positive feedback, noting that you&#x27;re both settling into a good routine, with cases being passed over and picked up efficiently . This smooth workflow is crucial for keeping things moving.</p><p>- **Solid Written Business:** You achieved £14.7k in February , and to stay on track for your £45k quarterly goal, you need to bring in approximately £15.3k more . You&#x27;re currently at 33% for Q4, and your year-to-date stats are at 45.5% .</p><p>### Key Areas for Focus &amp; Action:</p><p>1. **Optimizing Lead Conversion &amp; Reducing NPW:**</p><p>   - **The Challenge:** Last month, you had 21 leads, with 4 converting to business . However, a significant point of discussion was the 108 leads (out of 196 total since you started) that have moved into NPW statuses, representing about 55% of your total leads . This high NPW rate means a lot of potential is being lost, and it can lead to wasted time and reduced capacity for progressing active cases .</p><p>   - **Your Action:** I&#x27;d like you to dedicate some thought over the next week or two to identifying common traits among these NPW cases. Consider the lead source, how the appointments were booked, and the pre-qualification process. We need to understand why these leads aren&#x27;t progressing to reduce this leakage . Our ideal goal is to get you to 35-40 leads a month, with a 1 in 2 conversion rate for those you actually speak to .</p><p>2. **Enhancing CRM Discipline (Lead Source &amp; Workflow):**</p><p>   - **Lead Source Accuracy:** We identified an issue with the **Lindsay Boyle** case, where the lead source and staff name were incorrectly entered . This is critical because incorrect coding can lead to cases being excluded from reporting and, more importantly, can impact your payment .</p><p>   - **Your Action:** Please **NTU and re-add the Lindsay Boyle case immediately** with the correct lead source setup: &#x27;Introducers&#x27; as the type, &#x27;Ready and Go&#x27; as the specific code, the correct introducer branch, and your name (Roland Brownlee) as the staff name .</p><p>   - **Workflow Progression:** We also discussed the importance of consistently updating workflow stages on the platform. An overdue task from eight days ago was noted . The platform is designed to automate tasks for admin (like Megan) when you move stages forward. If you don&#x27;t, Megan won&#x27;t receive the system prompts to chase documents or handle next steps, causing delays .</p><p>   - **Your Action:** Make it a habit to **complete tasks and move workflow stages forward on the same day** after your appointments . This will ensure Megan gets the necessary triggers and allows you to focus on advising rather than administrative chasing .</p><p>3. **Accurate Protection &amp; On-Risk Dates:**</p><p>   - **The Challenge:** While your £19k in protection policies is good , we&#x27;re still missing on-risk dates for cases like **Claire Betts** and **Gary Linton**. This lack of information impacts the accuracy of your projected income reporting, as we can&#x27;t see when that income is expected to land .</p><p>   - **Your Action:** For any cases where the exact on-risk date isn&#x27;t known, please **add an estimated projected date**. As discussed, a good default is three months from now (e.g., **2026-06-04** for cases discussed today) . Megan can then refine this once confirmed by solicitors or other updates.</p><p>4. **Admin Fees Overview:**</p><p>   - **The Trend:** We observed a slight dip in admin fees during January and February, even though you wrote more business .</p><p>   - **Your Insight:** Your explanation about product transfers where you didn&#x27;t charge a fee but ensured insurance was attached makes perfect sense .</p><p>   - **Your Action:** Continue to monitor this, ensuring that unless it&#x27;s a very specific scenario (like your granny!), every case has appropriate attachments or fees .</p><p>5. **Remortgage Opportunities &amp; Future Pipeline:**</p><p>   - **The Opportunity:** It&#x27;s great that you&#x27;re proactively building rapport with clients for future remortgages. We have 6 cases for March and 7 for April coming from Stephen , which will realistically translate into business for next year. Beyond that, there are 80 applications noted for next year, representing a potential £30k worth of business .</p><p>   - **My Action:** I&#x27;ll ensure Anna (or Megan) starts calling these clients to get them booked into your diary, so you have a solid pipeline ready to go .</p><p>6. **Platform &amp; Teams Issues:**</p><p>   - **Platform Display:** Thanks for flagging the platform issue where face-to-face appointments are incorrectly showing Glasgow South Side and West End instead of Newton Mearns . This can cause client confusion.</p><p>   - **My Action:** I&#x27;ll raise a ticket for this to get it corrected, ensuring it only comes up as Newton Mearns .</p><p>   - **Teams Disruption:** Please keep me in the loop regarding the ongoing Teams issue that stemmed from the voicemail request .</p><p>### Your Priority List for the Coming Week:</p><p>1. **Immediate CRM Correction:****NTU and re-add the Lindsay Boyle case** with the correct lead source fields (Introducers, &quot;Ready and Go&quot; code, correct branch, and your staff name) .</p><p>2. **On-Risk Date Discipline:****Add estimated on-risk/projected dates** for Claire Betts and Gary Linton (using **2026-06-04** as a default estimate) and refine them as confirmed .</p><p>3. **Workflow &amp; Task Completion:** Make a conscious effort to **complete overdue tasks and move workflow stages forward** on the same day after appointments .</p><p>4. **NPW Analysis:** Start thinking about and **analyzing the common drivers behind the 55% NPW rate** to identify potential changes in our lead booking or qualification process .</p><p>5. **Teams Update:** Keep me posted on any developments or resolution regarding the Teams issue .</p><p>   You&#x27;re doing a fantastic job, Roland. This month is truly yours for the taking – let&#x27;s not let Paul trump you in the last week!</p><p>   Cheers,</p><p>   Bob</p><p>Full transcript</p><p>Megan Gordon: And the bomb. So we moved at the right time, at least.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, yeah. What were you saying?</p><p>Megan Gordon: He was asking.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: If I was on a personal flat. Cause they&#x27;re flat with all the baby gubbins that are pissing in the neck. That&#x27;s true.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Do you know at least you moved first, Megan? I remember when I know. When Karen was pregnant, we were on, in a flat in Gorgie on the fourth floor. Of course, she&#x27;s like six months pregnant, and I can&#x27;t help now, can I? I am like,&quot; Yeah, lucky you.&quot; I&#x27;ll go up and down four flights of stairs.</p><p>Megan Gordon: Yeah, yeah. No. Well, at least we&#x27;ve had a year. Well, over a year now actually. You don&#x27;t know.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, it&#x27;s been a while you&#x27;ve been in there, haven&#x27;t you?</p><p>Megan Gordon: Yeah. So we&#x27;ve had a bit of time to. Get the house somewhat how we wanted it first.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, and then it&#x27;s just going to get turned into a mess later. Yeah.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So I&#x27;m just having a bit of toast. That&#x27;s my breakfast and lunch that I&#x27;m having right now. Right, let me load up my list of. Talking points. So obviously beginning a new month. Let&#x27;s have a little look at where you finished. For the whole of last month, in regards to Leeds. And your name is Rollins, so let me find Rollins. So 21 leads in total. I&#x27;m just going to share my screen, just so you can see what I&#x27;m looking at as well. That&#x27;s always kind of helpful to be honest. So. This is just a full.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Breakdown of new appointments have come in so twenty one out out of total, out of those twenty one leads. Four parts or four opportunities turned into business, but that&#x27;s obviously not all the business that you rose last month. But it just says that out of the twenty one, four of those opportunities turned into actual business. One still at sign up, three as at offer received. And then just a breakdown of the overall leads as a whole. So we&#x27;ve still got a few set appointment booked, so might want to just double check on those. Um,</p><p>Roland Brownlee: In there this morning. They&#x27;re due uh today and Friday, so they are okay.</p><p>Bob Duncan: That&#x27;s fine. Um, and then obviously not too many on the NPW, which is quite nice. So we&#x27;ve just got the one in there, one client not interested and four where we&#x27;ve just gone into stale lead. So that is absolutely. Fine, so all looking good. What I will do is I am just going to dive into the detail. And Roland. Well, this isn&#x27;t going to give me Q3 as a whole, but that&#x27;s fine. We will look at And then.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So I&#x27;ve got a couple like created Megan. I wonder if these are B and C ones.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: I just submitted Boyle today.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Because I&#x27;ve got well, I&#x27;ve got prospect status created. And then that so this one doesn&#x27;t have a branch.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: Yeah, that was self- gen. That&#x27;s uh If today, just done it, just now.</p><p>Megan Gordon: Co co is a, BNC.</p><p>Bob Duncan: BNC right that&#x27;s fine and then Lindsay Boyle. You said it was a self- gen.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: Uh, yeah, I&#x27;ve just submitted it now. I&#x27;ve been chasing them for months and months and months.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Okay, so you submitted an application or.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: Yeah, it was a product transfer literally this morning.</p><p>Megan Gordon: It&#x27;s not showing a staff name because you&#x27;ve added it wrong, Roland. Sorry.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: Can you tell me that offline? What have you done? You&#x27;ve put extra so.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Do you want to share your screen and then just quickly show them? I&#x27;m going to eat another bit of toast.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: All right, I&#x27;m just going to write this do