fall. Yeah, So if you can just make sure that we&#x27;ve got even if it&#x27;s just an estimated date, I guess I normally go if I don&#x27;t have a date, I just say Sean stick it in three months down the line. Um. And then that way once Megan finds it or the solicitors confirm, you know, moving data is sooner than Megan, should be updating that and bringing it forward. So all your dates match.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: Yeah yeah you mentioned that last week as well.</p><p>Megan Gordon: I&#x27;ve just updated John Betts. So his is all done. Gary Linton, did you see that he was waiting on something?</p><p>Roland Brownlee: Um, no no.</p><p>Megan Gordon: Oh fine.</p><p>Roland Brownlee: Oh fine, cool accepted normal terms.</p><p>Bob Duncan: And then our exchange dates are all good on the mortgages, which is great And written by month, I am. Actually, disappointed Roland that Paul beat you. I actually can&#x27;t believe where Paul. I don&#x27;t even know where he pulled out of the bag, because at no point, did he mention that. He was going to be throwing on these huge policies. But there is always this month.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Absolutely, I know you know there is always this month and are. On track for your forty five k, which is quite nice. You know you&#x27;ve done fourteen seven in February. Another. Fi fteen k or close to fifteen, fifteen three to cover the shortfall, puts you on track for the quarter, which is quite nice. Yeah, yeah. You feel that you&#x27;ve got enough in there. The bag to get there,</p><p>Roland Brownlee: Um, don&#x27;t know is the answer. You know, it&#x27;s like being a mortgage advisor. You never come out. And you are like thinking,&quot; Well, where is next week coming right? Well, where is the week after coming from?&quot; But that&#x27;s the thing about AIPS and keeping in touch with them. They&#x27;ll just come out the blue when yeah, sometimes you get a wee cracker. So I had one of those this week. Okay, suddenly, we&#x27;ve bought a house now